<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-2-1-overview" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Chapter Overview</title>

  <p>
    With your development environment set up and an initial understanding of how
    computers execute instructions and store data in memory, it’s time to get
    programming! As you read through this chapter, keep both JShell and Eclipse open so
    you can code along with the examples. The most effective way to learn programming is
    by doing it, so ask questions, devise experiments, and try different variations on
    the provided code. You don’t have to accept anything this book tells you on faith. A
    programming language operates in a completely deterministic, predictable way, making
    JShell and Eclipse the ideal sandboxes for testing ideas until each concept is fully
    clear in your mind.
  </p>

  <p>
    This chapter focuses on getting comfortable with Java by writing programs that follow
    the classic Input <m>\rightarrow</m> Processing <m>\rightarrow</m> Output (IPO)
    pattern. Nearly every practical computer program follows this fundamental structure.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Input:</strong> Gathering data from the outside world. In this chapter,
        your programs will read numbers and text entered by the user at the keyboard using
        Java’s standard <c>Scanner</c> class.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Processing:</strong> Manipulating and transforming that data in memory.
        This involves storing values in named memory locations called variables,
        performing calculations with arithmetic operators, and working with both
        fundamental building-blocks (primitive types, such as integers and floating-point
        numbers) and text (called <q>strings</q> in programming).
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Output:</strong> Sending results back to the user. You will display
        formatted messages, calculations, and prompts to the console using Java's
        standard output tools (<c>System.out.print</c>, <c>System.out.println</c>, and
        <c>System.out.printf</c>).
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Understanding the IPO cycle provides an immediate framework for designing programs.
    Before writing a single line of code, train yourself to ask three basic questions:
    What data must go into the program? What calculations or operations must take place?
    What results must come out?
  </p>

  <p>
    To make these concepts stick, we will ground our explanations in enough hardware and
    memory specifics to demystify what is happening inside the computer. Rather than
    asking you to memorize programming rules without understanding them, we will look
    briefly behind the scenes at how values are organized in memory (the stack versus the
    heap) and how finite binary representations affect mathematical calculations. When
    you understand the physical reality of how the machine works, programming ceases to
    be an exercise in memorization, and common pitfalls—such as integer overflow or
    floating-point rounding discrepancies—become logical and easy to avoid.
  </p>

  <p>
    Alongside the language mechanics, we will also begin introducing core software
    engineering practices. Writing code that simply compiles and runs is only the
    baseline; professional developers write code that is well structured, readable, and
    easy to maintain. Throughout this chapter, you will learn standard Java conventions,
    including choosing descriptive variable names, applying standard capitalization rules
    like camelCase, and writing helpful comments to document your code's intent.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="pars-2-1-roadmap">
    <title>Roadmap for Chapter 2</title>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-2-2-app-overview" /> 
          Java application overview<mdash /> An overview of a
          complete, runnable Java application, examining classes, methods, and
          statements.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-2-3-class-structure" /> 
          Class structure<mdash /> How a Java program is put
          together.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-2-4-variables-types" /> 
          Data: variables, literals, and types<mdash />Exploring the
          distinction between statements and expressions, declaring variables, working
          with primitive types, and looking at how Java manages memory.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
           <xref ref="sec-2-5-arithmetic" /> 
          Arithmetic<mdash />Performing calculations, using the
          built-in <c>Math</c> library, understanding operator precedence, and avoiding
          finite binary pitfalls.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-2-6-strings" /> 
          Manipulating text: Working with strings<mdash />
          Understanding what a <c>String</c> is, exploring how objects differ from
          primitives in memory, navigating immutability, using escape sequences, and
          applying common <c>String</c> methods.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-2-7-output-system-out" /> 
          Output<mdash /> Working with console output, moving from
          simple unformatted text to aligned tables and formatted numbers.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-2-8-input-scanner" /> 
          Input: Reading from the console<mdash />Using the
          <c>Scanner</c> class to read interactive user input from the console, completing
          the full Input–Processing–Output cycle.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </paragraphs>

  <p>
    After you’ve mastered the concepts in this chapter, you’ll be writing your own Java
    programs that obtain user input and process it to produce formatted results, and you
    will have the foundational knowledge of Java memory management and binary
    representations to move forward with confidence.
  </p>
</section>